\section{Why this package cannot avoid it}{

OB.DAAC requires an Earthdata Login on every data access point. There is no
anonymous route, and the failure when you have not got one is unhelpful in a
particular way: the server answers \strong{HTTP 200} with the login page, so a
naive download writes nine kilobytes of HTML into a file called \code{.nc} and
the error surfaces later as a corrupt netCDF. \code{\link[=obdaac_download]{obdaac_download()}} checks the
bytes for this and says what actually happened.
}

\section{Setting one up}{

Register once at \url{https://urs.earthdata.nasa.gov/users/new}, then either:

\itemize{
\item \strong{An appkey}, which is the simpler of the two. Generate one at
\url{https://oceandata.sci.gsfc.nasa.gov/appkey/} and put it in
\code{options(datamatch.earthdata_appkey = "...")}, or in the
\code{EARTHDATA_APPKEY} environment variable. It is a bearer token, so keep
it out of scripts you share — \code{.Renviron} is the usual home.
\item \strong{A \verb{~/.netrc}}, which is what NASA's own examples use and what
other tools on your machine may already read:
\verb{machine urs.earthdata.nasa.gov login USERNAME password PASSWORD}.
Set it to mode 600. This needs \code{curl} on the \code{PATH}.
}

The appkey is preferred where both are present, because it needs no cookie
jar and no external program.
}

